4.5  fsync: when is data really on disk?

TL;DR
  - write() returning means the data reached the kernel's memory, not the disk.
    Linux writes it out later, usually within 30 seconds.
  - fsync(fd) waits until the file's data and metadata are on the device, and
    asks the drive to flush its own cache. It's the only promise.
  - To replace a file safely: write a temporary file, fsync it, rename it over
    the old one, then fsync the directory.

      grep -E '^(Dirty|Writeback):' /proc/meminfo   # not yet on disk
      sync                 # flush everything, and wait

On your machine
  nvme0n1   read 94.8 GiB in 2.8 million requests, 0.15 ms each; wrote 217.8 GiB
            in 5.1 million requests, 0.46 ms each; busy 1% of the time; 345,678
            cache flushes
  sda       read 396.7 MiB in 3,120 requests, 7.52 ms each; wrote 102.5 MiB in
            812 requests, 121.63 ms each; busy under 1% of the time
  Since     boot, 3 days, 4 h ago
  Pending   1.5 MiB not yet written, 0 B being written; data waits up to 30 s in
            RAM

  nvme0n1   Samsung SSD 980 PRO 500GB: NVMe flash, 476.9 GiB · scheduler none ·
            8 hardware queues · volatile write cache · TRIM
  sda       USB DISK 3.0: USB drive, 30.0 GiB · scheduler mq-deadline · no
            volatile write cache

Pitfalls
  - A new file isn't durable until its directory is fsynced too: after a crash,
    the data may exist but the name may not.
  - Most drives have a volatile write cache; fsync flushes it. Settings or VMs
    that ignore flushes ("unsafe" cache modes) trade durability for speed.
  - On a crash, ext4 (in its default mode) and XFS keep the filesystem
    consistent, not your file's latest contents: that's still fsync's job.
  - Calling fsync after every small write is slow; batch writes, then sync once.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 4.5 --why

